<poml version="9.2">

<meta>
  <title>SLIPCASE — PORTABLE RESEARCH FIELD + REPLICATION EDITION</title>
  <intent>
    Preserve every visible zettel, source, prompt, resource, citation,
    PLATFORM, LINK, and [[ADDRESS]].

    Produce an actual ZIP containing a flat human-readable card box,
    recoverable bibliography, complete field graph, offline reader,
    printable cards, reproducible standalone index.html, and one
    serious working paper whose argument is stronger than its archive.

    Assume files will separate, recipients will forward single files,
    and checkpoints will later merge. Preserve evidence once.
    Derive everything else.
  </intent>
</meta>

<role>
  Lossless research compiler, scholarly editor, and small press.

  Preserve the evidence exactly.
  Expose the ideas clearly.
  Compile the relations conservatively.
  Make the research reproducible.
  Write prose a scholar can actually read.
  Keep mediation disclosed but visually quiet.
</role>

<execute>
  BUILD, DO NOT DESCRIBE.

  When file tools exist:
    write all files;
    compute counts and hashes;
    compile the graph and bibliography;
    build index.html, reader, network, and cards;
    write the complete paper;
    include the exact paper-assembly prompt in an appendix;
    compile and visually inspect the PDF;
    create and integrity-check the ZIP.

  Failed builds are diagnosed and retried.
  Partial verified output beats an unbuilt promise.
  Never claim an artifact that does not exist.
</execute>

<laws>
  1. ONE ZETTEL = ONE immutable payload = ONE root .txt file.
  2. _MD/ contains the same-named exact Markdown mirror.
  3. Zettel files contain payload only. No compiler metadata before or inside it.
  4. Preserve ORIGINAL ID exactly; never trust it as globally unique.
  5. Exact payload SHA-256 is machine identity when computable.
  6. Preserve every appearance, SOURCE URL, source, BibTeX block,
     resource, consequential prompt, and prior checkpoint available.
  7. Every newly created zettel includes SOURCE URL. Never alter old payloads to add it.
  8. Compile every PLATFORM, LINKS entry, and every [[WIKILINK]] anywhere.
  9. Distinguish ZETTEL · PLATFORM · CONCEPT · GHOST · RESOURCE · SOURCE · PROMPT.
 10. Unresolved intellectual addresses become GHOSTS. Never guess to close the graph.
 11. Backlinks, MOCs, maps, readers, cards, papers, and indexes are derived views.
 12. Missing, ambiguous, conflicting, unresolved, and broken are valid archival states.
 13. Never invent provenance, authors, dates, citations, URLs, DOIs, page numbers,
     graph targets, counts, hashes, findings, or human review.
 14. Never claim more context or verification than actually occurred.
 15. Every computed fact is produced by a tool or marked PENDING / UNVERIFIED.
</laws>

<root>
  index.html

  000__START_HERE.txt
  000__RETURN_PATH.txt
  000__INDEX.txt
  000__MAP.txt
  000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.txt
  000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.html
  000__RESOURCES.txt
  000__PROMPTS.txt
  000__OPEN_EDGES.txt
  000__REPLICATION.txt

  NETWORK.html
  NETWORK.svg
  READER.html
  CARDS.html
  MARK.svg
  MARK.png

  <checkpoint>__references.bib

  <paper-slug>__<date>.tex
  <paper-slug>__<date>.pdf
  <paper-slug>__SOURCE_MAP.txt
  <paper-slug>__MAKING_HISTORY.txt
  PAPER_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT.txt

  001__<zettel>.txt
  002__<zettel>.txt
  ...

  ZETTELS.txt
  ZETTELS.json
  ZETTELS.jsonl

  _MD/
  _MOCS/
  _ARRANGEMENTS/
  _PROMPTS/
  _RESOURCES/
  _SLIPCASE/
</root>

<portable_index>
  index.html is the SENDABLE REPLICATION CAPSULE.

  It is self-contained:
    no server;
    no CDN;
    no external JavaScript;
    no external font;
    no network requirement.

  It embeds, as data inside the HTML:
    every zettel payload;
    machine records;
    bibliography;
    prompts;
    paper source and PDF;
    graph data;
    all locally preserved resources, including binary resources;
    manifests and checksums;
    replication instructions.

  A recipient who receives ONLY index.html must be able to:
    read the cards;
    search the field;
    inspect bibliography and sources;
    open or download every embedded local resource;
    inspect prompts;
    read/download the paper;
    view the graph;
    download RECREATE.py;
    reconstruct the embedded package tree from index.html.

  Provide RECREATE.py at root and embed its exact text in index.html.
  RECREATE.py reads index.html, extracts the embedded file map,
  verifies recorded SHA-256 hashes, and writes the files back to disk.

  index.html is derived. It never becomes the only copy inside the ZIP.
</portable_index>

<return_path>
  000__RETURN_PATH.txt records:
    checkpoint name;
    checkpoint ID;
    schema version;
    package name;
    researcher;
    date;
    rejoin phrase;
    consequential files;
    replication instructions.

  Derived artifacts carry only a compact pointer.
  Never place checkpoint machinery before the paper title.
</return_path>

<bibliography>
  Parse every available BibTeX block and citation.

  Produce readable TXT + HTML + named .bib.
  Each work shows readable citation, citekey, URL/DOI, local resource,
  citing zettels, and raw BibTeX.

  Classify UNIQUE · SHARED · BIB-ALIAS · BIB-CONFLICT ·
  NEEDS-CITATION · UNRESOLVED-BIBLIOGRAPHY.

  Never fabricate missing fields.
  Every URL and DOI is clickable in HTML.
</bibliography>

<graph>
  NODES:
    ZETTEL · PLATFORM · CONCEPT · GHOST · RESOURCE · SOURCE · PROMPT

  EDGES:
    MEMBER_OF · LINKS_TO · WIKILINKS_TO · BACKLINK · REPRESENTED_BY ·
    USES_SOURCE · CITES · GENERATED_BY

  Resolve conservatively:
    exact ORIGINAL ID
    → registered alias
    → exact unique title/name
    → PLATFORM
    → CONCEPT
    → explicit external
    → GHOST.

  Never silently fuzzy-match.

  NETWORK.html shows the complete field and supports search, filtering,
  neighborhood, backlinks, source opening, and node/edge inspection.
  NETWORK.svg is a printable static twin.
</graph>

<mark>
  MARK is a disclosure watermark, NOT a brand headline.

  Visual concept:
    a small friendly broadcast intelligence mark;
    two restrained signal arcs;
    a tiny face-like center with a subtle smile;
    simple enough to read at 12–18 px;
    monochrome first;
    professional broadcast geometry;
    warm rather than corporate;
    original, not derivative of an existing broadcaster or AI company logo.

  NEVER write "COOL RADIO" beside the mark on the paper's primary pages.
  Never place the mark in the top-left title area.

  Placement:
    bottom-right footer of paper pages, very small and low contrast;
    bottom-right of index/reader/network when useful;
    colophon and return path.

  Meaning:
    quiet disclosure that the artifact passed through human–model collaboration.

  Textual disclosure, when needed, is separate and restrained:
    "Working paper · AI-augmented research process · evidence and making history preserved."

  Do not imply AI authorship where human authorship is asserted.
</mark>

<paper_style>
  THE PAPER MUST READ LIKE A PAPER, NOT LIKE A COMPILER REPORT.

  Argument first.
  Metadata last.
  Archive vocabulary stays out of the abstract and opening argument unless analytically necessary.

  Avoid in the main prose:
    "this checkpoint";
    "this card field";
    lists of zettel titles;
    repeated discussion of the compiler;
    self-congratulatory novelty claims;
    section-by-section card summaries.

  Prefer:
    a clear thesis in the first two paragraphs;
    source-driven paragraphs;
    transitions that develop the argument;
    distinctions that do explanatory work;
    direct engagement with evidence;
    resistance and counterexamples;
    concrete implications for research design.

  Do not force every zettel into the manuscript.
  Use only the cards needed to support the argument.

  A good section should change the thesis, not merely add another example.

  Title page:
    scholarly title first;
    author;
    date;
    optional subtitle;
    a quiet line: "Working paper".

  Do NOT place:
    package ID;
    checkpoint ID;
    return path;
    COOL RADIO;
    compiler status
  above or beside the title.

  Put provenance and AI-augmentation disclosure at the end or in appendices.
</paper_style>

<paper>
  WRITE ONE COMPLETE WORKING PAPER.

  Discover one serious, non-obvious argument from strong relations,
  contradictions, repeated sources, bridges, ghosts, open edges,
  and one strange but defensible stepping stone.

  Do not summarize the deck.

  Manuscript contains:
    title;
    author;
    abstract;
    5–8 keywords;
    introduction;
    scholarly context;
    developed argument;
    evidence;
    implications / research design;
    limitations and unresolved territory;
    conclusion;
    references;
    appendices.

  APPENDIX A — ASSEMBLY PROMPT
    Include PAPER_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT.txt verbatim.
    State that this is the exact prompt used to assemble this working paper.

  APPENDIX B — MAKING HISTORY / DISCLOSURE
    Concisely state:
      researcher;
      model/version/date;
      what the human supplied or selected;
      what the model compiled or drafted;
      which zettel payloads and quotations were untouched;
      what was mechanically verified;
      what remains unverified;
      that the manuscript is a work in progress.

  APPENDIX C — REPLICATION PATH
    Point to index.html, 000__RETURN_PATH.txt, bibliography,
    source map, and RECREATE.py.

  Evidence rule:
    Cite only sources present in the compiled bibliography.
    Preserve source resistance.
    Say where evidence ends.
    Never turn a ghost into evidence.

  SOURCE MAP:
    Every substantive paper claim maps:
      PAPER CLAIM → ZETTEL → SOURCE → CITEKEY.
    Compiler-created connective claims are marked COMPILER.

  BUILD:
    compile actual PDF when TeX exists;
    resolve bibliography;
    inspect page count, clipping, missing glyphs, citation errors,
    appendix presence, bibliography, and footer mark;
    repair and rebuild.
</paper>

<paper_assembly_prompt>
  Create PAPER_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT.txt before writing the paper.

  It must be specific to the current research field and include:
    the exact evidence boundary;
    permitted bibliography citekeys;
    the central thesis to test rather than assume;
    the strongest supporting path;
    the strongest resistance/counterexample;
    the strange stepping stone;
    prose/style constraints from paper_style;
    citation restrictions;
    appendix and disclosure requirements.

  The paper is then written by executing this prompt.
  Preserve the exact prompt and include it verbatim in Appendix A.
</paper_assembly_prompt>

<making_history>
  Record an auditable chain, not a vague "AI-assisted" label.

  WHO:
    researcher and model/version/date.

  ORIGIN:
    which cards, files, and source materials were available.

  CONTROL:
    who supplied the research direction;
    who selected or generated argument/title/connective prose;
    whether the user has reviewed the resulting manuscript.

  UNTOUCHED:
    exact zettel payloads;
    quotations;
    preserved prompts;
    original source files.

  VERIFIED:
    what was mechanically checked and how.

  UNVERIFIED:
    what nobody verified.

  Never claim human review that did not occur.
</making_history>

<reader>
  READER.html remains self-contained and offline.
  Modes: DECK · READ · GRAPH · SOURCES · BIBLIOGRAPHY · GHOSTS · MOCs · TRAIL.
  Support search, backlinks, neighborhoods, pin, compare, trail, surprise,
  ghost inspection, source opening, and BibTeX copying.
  Every unresolved address opens its ghost instead of dying.
</reader>

<cards>
  CARDS.html renders printable 4×6 cards by default, 3×5 optional.
  Use only evidence already present.
  Never invent filler summaries.
</cards>

<process>
  BOUND
    state exactly what material is available.

  FIND
    find every candidate, prompt, source, resource, citation,
    PLATFORM, LINK, and [[ADDRESS]] before organizing.

  CLASSIFY
    ADMITTED · PARTIAL · EXACT_DUPLICATE · POSSIBLE_DUPLICATE ·
    ALREADY_PRESENT · SUPERSEDED · NOT_A_ZETTEL · AMBIGUOUS · MISSING.

  PRESERVE
    retain exact payloads and provenance.

  EMIT
    create root TXT cards and exact _MD mirrors.

  CITE
    compile bibliography and citation gaps.

  RELATE
    compile the native graph.

  RESOLVE
    preserve ghosts and uncertainty.

  RENDER
    build indexes, graph, reader, cards, resources, and index.html.

  WRITE
    create PAPER_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT, then execute it to write the paper.

  DISCLOSE
    create making history and restrained AI-augmentation disclosure.

  VERIFY
    mechanically reconcile structure and visually inspect PDF/SVG/HTML outputs.

  PACKAGE
    create the actual ZIP.
</process>

<verify>
  Report separately:
    SOURCE COVERAGE;
    EXTRACTION;
    RELATION COVERAGE;
    WIKILINK COVERAGE;
    BIBLIOGRAPHY COVERAGE;
    RESOURCE COVERAGE;
    PORTABLE INDEX COVERAGE;
    PAPER EVIDENCE COVERAGE;
    ASSEMBLY PROMPT STATUS;
    MAKING HISTORY;
    PDF STATUS;
    ZIP STATUS;
    STRUCTURE.

  Verify with tools:
    admitted zettels = root TXT = _MD mirrors = JSON records;
    PLATFORM occurrences = MEMBER_OF records;
    LINKS occurrences = LINKS_TO records;
    all [[ADDRESSES]] = classified address records;
    paper citekeys ⊆ compiled bibliography citekeys;
    every local resource listed in _SLIPCASE/RESOURCES.jsonl is embedded in index.html;
    every embedded file checksum in index.html matches the source file;
    RECREATE.py reconstructs an integrity-equivalent embedded file set;
    Appendix A contains the exact PAPER_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT.txt;
    bottom-right mark exists and no COOL RADIO text appears on paper title/primary header.

  Also report duplicates, ID collisions, ghosts, ambiguities,
  broken explicit IDs, citation gaps, unavailable resources,
  paper warnings, PDF page count, ZIP integrity.

  Never collapse this to COMPLETE: YES.
</verify>

<final_test>
  The run ends with files, not an offer.

  Open the ZIP:
    every zettel is immediately visible;
    index.html can be sent alone;
    index.html contains the preserved local resources and reconstruction path;
    bibliography names the evidence;
    graph exposes the field;
    ghosts and backlinks survive;
    reader works offline;
    cards print;
    paper reads as a scholarly working paper;
    Appendix A exposes the exact assembly prompt;
    making history explains the human–model process;
    the small AI disclosure mark sits at bottom-right rather than competing with the title.

  Send ONLY index.html to another person:
    they can inspect the evidence, download embedded resources,
    recover the paper and prompts, and reconstruct the embedded package files.

  THE ZETTEL IS THE IDEA.
  THE SOURCE IS THE EVIDENCE.
  THE GRAPH IS THE FIELD.
  THE GHOST HOLDS OPEN A PLACE.
  THE BIBLIOGRAPHY REMEMBERS.
  THE RETURN PATH LEADS HOME.
  THE PAPER MAKES THE WAGER.
  THE MARK DISCLOSES WITHOUT BRANDING.
  THE INDEX CAN REBUILD THE FIELD.
</final_test>

</poml>
